    <main id="main">
        <div class="wrap page">
            <div class="tag">Security</div>
            <h1>Security Whitepaper</h1>
            <p class="subtitle">How DistLLM protects your data, infrastructure, and inference workloads at every layer.</p>

            <div class="grid-2">
                <div class="sec-card">
                    <div class="sec-card-icon">&#128274;</div>
                    <h3>Authentication</h3>
                    <p>Multiple authentication methods for different trust levels.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>API Keys</strong> — Scoped keys with configurable permissions and expiration</li>
                        <li><strong>mTLS</strong> — Mutual TLS for worker-to-coordinator and coordinator-to-client</li>
                        <li><strong>Cluster Keys</strong> — Shared secrets for cluster node authentication</li>
                        <li><strong>JWT Tokens</strong> — Short-lived tokens for session-based access</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="sec-card">
                    <div class="sec-card-icon">&#128272;</div>
                    <h3>Encryption</h3>
                    <p>End-to-end encryption for all data in transit and at rest.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>TLS 1.3</strong> — All API and inter-node communication encrypted</li>
                        <li><strong>E2E Encryption</strong> — Request/response payloads encrypted client-to-worker</li>
                        <li><strong>KV Cache Encryption</strong> — In-memory cache encrypted with per-session keys</li>
                        <li><strong>At-Rest</strong> — Model weights and configs encrypted on disk (optional)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="sec-card">
                    <div class="sec-card-icon">&#128101;</div>
                    <h3>Access Control</h3>
                    <p>Role-based access control with fine-grained permissions.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>RBAC</strong> — Admin, Operator, Developer, and Viewer roles</li>
                        <li><strong>Model ACLs</strong> — Per-model access restrictions</li>
                        <li><strong>Rate Limiting</strong> — Token bucket per-user, per-model rate limits</li>
                        <li><strong>IP Allowlists</strong> — Restrict access by IP range or CIDR block</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div class="sec-card">
                    <div class="sec-card-icon">&#128221;</div>
                    <h3>Audit Logging</h3>
                    <p>Comprehensive audit trail for all operations.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Request Logging</strong> — Every API call logged with user, model, timestamp</li>
                        <li><strong>Admin Actions</strong> — Configuration changes, key rotations, node events</li>
                        <li><strong>Export Formats</strong> — JSON, syslog, OpenTelemetry export</li>
                        <li><strong>Retention</strong> — Configurable retention with automatic rotation</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2>Compliance</h2>
            <p>DistLLM is designed to help organizations meet regulatory requirements for AI workloads.</p>
            <div class="grid-2">
                <div class="sec-card">
                    <span class="badge badge-green">SOC 2 Ready</span>
                    <h3>SOC 2 Type II</h3>
                    <p>DistLLM's architecture supports SOC 2 compliance requirements including access controls, audit logging, encryption, and incident response. Self-hosted deployment keeps data within your perimeter.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="sec-card">
                    <span class="badge badge-green">GDPR Compliant</span>
                    <h3>GDPR</h3>
                    <p>Data stays on your infrastructure by default. No telemetry, no phone-home, no third-party data sharing. Full data residency control for EU requirements.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h2>Vulnerability Reporting</h2>
            <p>We take security seriously. If you discover a vulnerability in DistLLM, please report it responsibly.</p>
            <div class="vuln-box">
                <h3>How to Report</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Email: <a href="mailto:security@distllm.dev">security@distllm.dev</a></li>
                    <li>PGP key available at <a href="https://distllm.dev/pgp-key.txt">distllm.dev/pgp-key.txt</a></li>
                    <li>GitHub Security Advisories for private disclosure</li>
                </ul>
                <h3>Response Timeline</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Acknowledgment:</strong> Within 24 hours</li>
                    <li><strong>Initial assessment:</strong> Within 72 hours</li>
                    <li><strong>Fix timeline:</strong> Critical: 7 days, High: 14 days, Medium: 30 days</li>
                    <li><strong>CVE assignment:</strong> Coordinated with reporters</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="badge badge-amber">Bug Bounty</span> We offer bounties for qualifying vulnerabilities. See our <a href="https://github.com/distributed-llm/distributed-llm/security/policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">security policy</a> for scope and rewards.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>
    <footer>
